---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import VectorsSim from '../../simulators/vectors/VectorsSim.astro';
import { vectorsQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/vectors';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What is a vector?' },
  { id: 'adding', label: 'Adding vectors graphically' },
  { id: 'components', label: 'Adding with components' },
  { id: 'subtracting', label: 'Subtracting vectors' },
  { id: 'formulas', label: 'Formulas' },
  { id: 'example', label: 'Worked example' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Ling, S. J., Sanny, J., & Moebs, W. (2016). University Physics Volume 1, Section 2.1: Scalars and Vectors. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/2-1-scalars-and-vectors' },
  { text: 'Ling, S. J., Sanny, J., & Moebs, W. (2016). University Physics Volume 1, Section 2.2: Coordinate Systems and Components of a Vector. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/2-2-coordinate-systems-and-components-of-a-vector' },
  { text: 'Abramson, J. (2021). Precalculus 2e, Section 8.8: Vectors. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/precalculus-2e/pages/8-8-vectors' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="mathematics"
  topic="vector-addition"
  description="Interactive vector addition simulator: drag two vectors, add them tip-to-tail or with a parallelogram, subtract them, and see magnitudes, directions and components."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={vectorsQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Walk 3 km east and then 4 km north, and you end up 5 km from where you started — not 7. Quantities that have a
      direction, like displacement, velocity and force, do not add like ordinary numbers. Drag two arrows and discover
      the rules for combining them.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <VectorsSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Drag the circles</strong> at the tips of vector A (teal) and vector B (purple), or set their size and direction with the sliders.</li>
      <li>The orange arrow is the <strong>resultant</strong> R. Its length, direction and components appear below the grid.</li>
      <li>Switch <strong>method</strong> to compare tip-to-tail and parallelogram addition — they always give the same R — or to see subtraction A − B.</li>
      <li>Try the <strong>Same direction</strong>, <strong>Opposite</strong> and <strong>Perpendicular</strong> buttons, and turn on <strong>components</strong> to see the x and y parts.</li>
      <li><strong>Reset</strong> restores the starting vectors.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="what">What is a vector?</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>scalar</strong> is a quantity with size only, such as mass (5 kg) or temperature (20 °C). A
    <strong>vector</strong> has both <strong>magnitude</strong> (size) and <strong>direction</strong>, such as a
    displacement of 5 km north-east or a force of 10 N downward. We draw a vector as an arrow: its length shows the
    magnitude and its orientation shows the direction. Moving an arrow without rotating or resizing it does not change
    the vector.
  </p>

  <h2 id="adding">Adding vectors graphically</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tip-to-tail method:</strong> draw A, then start B at the tip of A. The resultant runs from the tail of A to the tip of B.</li>
    <li><strong>Parallelogram method:</strong> draw A and B from the same starting point and complete the parallelogram. The diagonal from the shared start is the resultant.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Both methods always give the same answer, and the order does not matter: A + B = B + A. The size of the resultant
    depends on the angle between the vectors — largest when they point the same way, smallest when they point in
    opposite directions.
  </p>

  <h2 id="components">Adding with components</h2>
  <p>
    Any vector can be split into an <strong>x-component</strong> and a <strong>y-component</strong> — how far it goes
    across and how far up. Adding vectors then becomes simple arithmetic: add all the x-components, add all the
    y-components, and rebuild the resultant from the totals. This is how scientists and engineers add many forces or
    velocities at once.
  </p>

  <h2 id="subtracting">Subtracting vectors</h2>
  <p>
    The negative of a vector, −B, has the same length as B but points the opposite way. To subtract, add the negative:
    A − B = A + (−B). When A and B start at the same point, A − B is the arrow from the tip of B to the tip of A. This is
    how you find a change in velocity or the position of one object relative to another.
  </p>

  <h2 id="formulas">Formulas</h2>
  <code class="formula">Components:  vₓ = |v| cos θ     v_y = |v| sin θ</code>
  <code class="formula">Sum:  R = (Aₓ + Bₓ,  A_y + B_y)</code>
  <code class="formula">Magnitude:  |R| = √(Rₓ² + R_y²)     Direction:  θ = tan⁻¹(R_y / Rₓ)  (check the quadrant)</code>
  <p>For two vectors with angle φ between them, the law of cosines gives:</p>
  <code class="formula">|A + B|² = |A|² + |B|² + 2|A||B| cos φ</code>

  <h2 id="example">Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    A student walks 3 km east and then 4 km north. What is their displacement from the start?
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write each step as components: <strong>a</strong> = (3, 0) and <strong>b</strong> = (0, 4), with x east and y north.</li>
    <li>Add the components: <strong>a</strong> + <strong>b</strong> = (3 + 0, 0 + 4) = (3, 4).</li>
    <li>Find the magnitude with Pythagoras: |<strong>a</strong> + <strong>b</strong>| = √(3² + 4²) = √25 = 5 km.</li>
    <li>Find the direction: θ = tan⁻¹(4 / 3) ≈ 53.1° north of east.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The student walked 7 km in total, but their displacement is only 5 km. Distance adds up every step; displacement
    is a vector, so steps in different directions partly cancel. Try it in the simulator by setting the two vectors to
    (3, 0) and (0, 4).
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Magnitude</dt>
    <dd>The size or length of a vector, always positive or zero.</dd>
    <dt>Resultant</dt>
    <dd>The single vector that has the same effect as two or more vectors combined.</dd>
    <dt>Component</dt>
    <dd>The part of a vector along one axis.</dd>
    <dt>Unit vector</dt>
    <dd>A vector of length 1 that indicates a direction only.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Navigation:</strong> pilots and sailors add their own velocity to the wind or current velocity to find their true path over the ground.</li>
    <li><strong>Forces:</strong> engineers add all the forces on a bridge or building to check that they balance.</li>
    <li><strong>Sport:</strong> a swimmer crossing a river ends up downstream because their velocity adds to the river’s.</li>
    <li><strong>Computer graphics and games:</strong> positions, movement and lighting are calculated with vector arithmetic.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“The resultant’s length is the sum of the lengths.”</strong> Only when the vectors point in exactly the same direction.</li>
    <li><strong>“Moving an arrow changes the vector.”</strong> A vector is defined by size and direction, not position.</li>
    <li><strong>“tan⁻¹ always gives the right angle.”</strong> It only returns angles between −90° and 90°; you must check which quadrant the vector is in.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Vectors have magnitude and direction and add tip-to-tail.</li>
    <li>Adding components separately is the most reliable method.</li>
    <li>The resultant is between |A| − |B| and |A| + |B|, depending on the angle.</li>
    <li>Subtraction means adding the opposite vector.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
